els":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"## How the puzzle works\n\n**What the grid contains.** Every grid has a background colour, two or three larger comb-shaped objects (colour 1, 4 or 8), and a scatter of red (colour 2) pieces. The pieces are single cells, 2×2 blocks, 2×3 blocks and so on.\n\n**What a comb looks like.** Each comb has a long bar with two outer legs. Between the legs sits a short inner stub. The empty space beside that stub is a slot facing outward. Some examples from the training pairs:\n- **Pair 3, top comb:** the bar is row 2. The stub is the single cell (3,10). The slot is column 10, below the stub.\n- **Pair 3, bottom comb:** the stub is (11,4..6), which is 3 wide. The slot is x 4..6 at rows 12..13.\n- **Pair 1, left comb:** the stub is the short prong (6,6). The slot opens to the right along row 6.\n\n**What happens to the red pieces.**\n- A red piece that lines up exactly with a slot slides straight into it. Its front stops against the stub.\n  - Pair 3: the 2×3 block at rows 16–17, x 4..6 moves up to rows 12–13.\n  - Pair 1: the single cell at (6,15) moves left to (6,7).\n  - Pair 2: the 2×2 block at rows 1–2 moves down to rows 9–10. It lands in the slot above the stub at (11,15..16).\n- The whole path the piece swept becomes **0**: every cell from just behind its new position back through its original cells.\n- Every red piece that has no slot to slide into disappears and becomes background.\n\n**How a \"matching slot\" is decided.** For each piece and each of the 4 directions:\n1. Slide the piece step by step while the cells in front of it are empty, ignoring other red cells. Stop when its front face would touch a comb.\n2. Accept the move only if all three of these hold:\n   - every cell directly in front of the face is comb, so the face is fully covered;\n   - the two cells just past the ends of that front strip are background, so the stub is exactly as wide as the piece;\n   - the piece's final cells lie inside the comb's bounding box, so it is inside a slot and not resting against the outside of the bar or a leg tip.\n\nThe second and third conditions rule out false matches. Example: in pair 1 the single cell at (1,13) would fall onto the comb's bar at row 11. The neighbours of (11,13) are comb cells, not background, and the stopping point is outside the box, so it is rejected.\n\n**Applying it to the test input.**\n- The cell at (11,17) rises into the top comb's slot. It sits against the stub at (4,17), lands at (5,17), and leaves 0s on rows 6..11.\n- The 3×2 block at rows 9–11, x 2–3 slides right into the left-facing slot of the vertical comb. It lands at x 7–8 against the 3-tall stub at x 9, leaving 0s on x 2..6.\n- The 2×2 block at rows 9–10, x 22–23 drops into the bottom comb's slot above the stub (19,22..23). It lands at rows 17–18, leaving 0s on rows 9..16.\n- All other red pieces vanish.\n\n**Teaching stages emitted by the program:**\n1. Every red piece is erased to background.\n2. One stage per piece that has a matching slot: its trail is painted 0 and the piece is stamped in its slot.\n3. The final canvas.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    red = 2\n    shapes = [o for o in objects(g, bg=bg) if o[\"colour\"] not in (bg, red)]\n    shape_mask = np.zeros_like(g, dtype=bool)\n    sid = -np.ones_like(g)\n    for i, o in enumerate(shapes):\n        for (y, x) in o[\"cells\"]:\n            shape_mask[y, x] = True\n            sid[y, x] = i\n    pieces = [o for o in objects(g, bg=bg) if o[\"colour\"] == red]\n\n    out = g.copy()\n    out[g == red] = bg\n    emit(out.copy(), \"Remove all red pieces; only those that fit a slot will be put back.\")\n\n    def inside(y, x):\n        return 0 <= y < H and 0 <= x < W\n\n    for p in pieces:\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = p[\"box\"]\n        cells = set(p[\"cells\"])\n        if len(cells) != (y1 - y0 + 1) * (x1 - x0 + 1):\n            continue\n        placed = False\n        for dy, dx in [(-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)]:\n            k = 0\n            ok = True\n            while True:\n                nxt = [(y + dy * (k + 1), x + dx * (k + 1)) for (y, x) in cells]\n                if any(not inside(y, x) for (y, x) in nxt):\n                    ok = False\n                    break\n                if any(shape_mask[y, x] for (y, x) in nxt):\n                    break\n                k += 1\n            if not ok or k == 0:\n                continue\n            final = [(y + dy * k, x + dx * k) for (y, x) in cells]\n            # front strip: cells directly beyond the leading face\n            if dy:\n                fy = (y0 if dy < 0 else y1) + dy * k + dy\n                front = [(fy, x) for x in range(x0, x1 + 1)]\n                ends = [(fy, x0 - 1), (fy, x1 + 1)]\n            else:\n                fx = (x0 if dx < 0 else x1) + dx * k + dx\n                front = [(y, fx) for y in range(y0, y1 + 1)]\n                ends = [(y0 - 1, fx), (y1 + 1, fx)]\n            if not all(inside(y, x) and shape_mask[y, x] for (y, x) in front):\n                continue\n            ids = {sid[y, x] for (y, x) in front}\n            if len(ids) != 1:\n                continue\n            if any(inside(y, x) and shape_mask[y, x] for (y, x) in ends):\n                continue\n            sb = shapes[ids.pop()][\"box\"]\n            if not all(sb[0] <= y <= sb[2] and sb[1] <= x <= sb[3] for (y, x) in final):\n                continue\n            # paint trail (all swept cells incl. original) with 0, then piece\n            for s in range(0, k + 1):\n                for (y, x) in cells:\n                    out[y + dy * s, x + dx * s] = 0\n            for (y, x) in final:\n                out[y, x] = red\n            emit(out.copy(), f\"Slide red piece at box {p['box']} into the matching slot, leaving a 0 trail.\")\n            placed = True\n            break\n    emit(out.copy(), \"Final grid.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"movement":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- A red piece that lines up exactly with a slot slides straight into it."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Slide the piece step by step while the cells in front of it are empty, ignoring other red cells."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- The 3×2 block at rows 9–11, x 2–3 slides right into the left-facing slot of the vertical comb."}],"template":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"One stage per piece that has a matching slot: its trail is painted 0 and the piece is stamped in its slot."}],"paths":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- The whole path the piece swept becomes **0**: every cell from just behind its new position back through its original cells."}],"dynamics":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Example: in pair 1 the single cell at (1,13) would fall onto the comb's bar at row 11."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- the piece's final cells lie inside the comb's bounding box, so it is inside a slot and not resting against the outside of the bar or a leg tip."}],"assembly":[{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Remove all red pieces; only those that fit a slot will be put back."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"6c92af9763ac7de870240457ebcdba3e7f8d8f20a4bf27fa7adc5b28080d9acb","task_sha256":"724acc0063a050e8dc4c0514a60cb771675287d905ea53637c2a800c6f0172b0","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
